meta {
  name: 9d2. Get api-access Token (public client, no secret)
  type: http
  seq: 26
}

post {
  url: {{keycloakUrl}}/realms/{{realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  grant_type: password
  client_id: {{apiAccessClientId}}
  username: {{username}}
  password: {{password}}
}

script:post-response {
  // Hand the bearer to the protected named-API reads in this folder.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200 && res.getBody() && res.getBody().access_token) {
    bru.setVar('sagaApiAccessToken', res.getBody().access_token);
  }
}

tests {
  test("api-access issues a token without a client secret", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("Response carries an access_token", function() {
    expect(res.body).to.have.property('access_token');
  });
}

docs {
  **Saga step 9d2 of 18** — Mint the external-consumer bearer.

  Protected named-API routes (`/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}` while the dataset is not open data) require a
  bearer token. Per concept #1293 an external API consumer obtains that token from the static **public**
  Keycloak client `api-access` — no client secret, Resource Owner Password Credentials (direct access
  grants) — not from the confidential `portal-frontend` client whose secret belongs to the portal UI and
  the management API. The request body therefore carries no `client_secret`.

  **Why this folder mints its own token:** the collection's `payload-api` folder already performs the same
  exchange and stores `apiAccessToken`, but folders run in alphabetical order and `dataset-saga-workflow`
  sorts **before** `payload-api`. `apiAccessToken` is still unset while this folder runs, so the protected
  reads below would send an empty bearer. This request stores its own `sagaApiAccessToken` instead.

  A valid token only clears the authentication gate — which datasets it may read is still an OPA decision
  on the gateway.

  **Precondition:** Keycloak reachable at `keycloakUrl`; realm `realm` has the public `api-access` client
  with direct access grants enabled; `username` / `password` identify a user with read access to the
  suite's dataset.

  **Expected Response:** 200 OK with an `access_token`.

  **Post-condition:** `sagaApiAccessToken` is set for the protected reads in steps 4 and 5.
}
